# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the P3.4 S11 sanitizer runs (TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<this file>).
# Only upstream, JUCE and system-library reports; never MCP code. Each line says why.
# Run with detect_deadlocks=0 as well: with libstdc++ std::mutex (trivial destructor, no pthread_mutex_destroy)
# reused heap addresses confuse TSan's deadlock detector ("double lock ... Mutex is already destroyed" on JUCE's
# WaitableEvent and on mcp::OneShotHandoff, whose state is only touched under its own mutex); the detector's
# per-thread lock list then overflows and aborts the run (CHECK n_all_locks_ < 64, seen in S11).

# moodycamel::ReaderWriterQueue synchronises with std::atomic_thread_fence, which TSan does not model; the
# queues are used single-producer/single-consumer (audio tick -> bridge timer), the queue's documented contract.
race:moodycamel::ReaderWriterQueue

# PulseAudio's client library internals (system library, not built by us).
called_from_lib:libpulsecommon-15.99.so
called_from_lib:libpulse.so.0

# JUCE TimeSliceThread lock vs the WaitableEvent mutex a condition wait releases (JUCE internal; seen through
# AudioFormatWriter::ThreadedWriter in the recorder).
deadlock:juce::TimeSliceThread
